// WHY THIS MODULE EXISTS, and why one lease is not the whole answer.
//
// A repository convergence on a live host is a multi-step mutation -- import objects, compare and
// swap the base ref, transition the worktree, read back. `git update-ref` gives compare-and-swap
// over the ref IT owns and nothing more: it does not span the later worktree transition. So there
// is no ordering of those steps under which every intermediate state is safe for another actuator
// to observe. If the swap succeeds and the worktree transition then fails, the base ref stands
// ADVANCED over an untransitioned tree, and `git worktree add` resolves through that ref -- which
// is exactly what the roadmap belt does every sixty seconds.
//
// TWO MECHANISMS, BECAUSE THEY ANSWER DIFFERENT QUESTIONS. The LEASE is mutual exclusion between
// ACTUATORS and is scoped to a process's critical section: it says someone is acting right now. The
// INHIBITION is a durable statement about the REPOSITORY and outlives every process that touches
// it: it says this tree is mid-transition, or was left mid-transition by something that died. A
// lease alone evaporates with the process that held it, and a process that dies between the swap
// and the readback is precisely the case the belt must keep refusing through. That is why the
// inhibition is a file whose PRESENCE is the refusal, and why a partial transition LEAVES IT SET.
//
// THE LEASE IS A HOLD, NOT A CHECK-THEN-WRITE AND NO LONGER A HAND LOCK. It is
// std.durable_exclusive_hold realized by gunbc.durable_exclusive_hold_file_store, reached only
// through gunbc.deploy_transition_lease_store: the acquire reads the slot, admits only absent or free,
// and publishes the next generation with an exclusive create, so two racing acquirers cannot both
// win; the release is the holder's own, compare-and-set on the generation it acquired. Reading first
// and writing if absent would be the same race this module exists to close, reintroduced inside it.
//
// FAIL-CLOSED IN BOTH DIRECTIONS. A lease slot that does not decode does NOT mean the lease is free,
// and an inhibition record that does not decode does NOT mean actuation is permitted: both answer
// their own refusal arm carrying the decode cause. Rendering either as
// "nothing found, proceed" is the empty-observation narrow -- could-not-read reported as
// nothing-is-wrong -- and it fails toward the exact interleaving the module forbids.
//
// WHAT THIS MODULE DOES NOT DO. It performs no git operation and imports no git authority, so it
// can be consulted by a belt tick without dragging a repository observation into a path that only
// wants to know whether it may act. Every repository fact it consumes -- the observed head, the
// candidate -- is supplied by the caller that already took that observation.

// WHAT THIS PAIR DOES NOT CLOSE, DECLARED RATHER THAN LEFT TO BE REDISCOVERED. The lease serializes
// CONVERGES against each other: it is the only ingress-independent mechanism, so an out-of-band
// `gunbc.apply` on the host and a workflow-dispatched deployment contend for the same slot and one
// of them refuses. It does NOT serialize a converge against a release-consuming actuator, because
// the belt does not take it -- the belt is held off by the INHIBITION, which is a durable statement
// about the repository rather than a claim on a critical section.
//
// The residual is therefore one window, and it is bounded and named: a belt tick that reads the
// standing as permitted, and a converge that sets the inhibition immediately afterwards, can overlap
// for the length of that tick's actuation batch. RUNG: *mitigatable*. The window is narrow (the
// standing is re-read at the actuation seam rather than at the top of the tick), and its direction
// on the next tick is safe -- the inhibition is set by then and the belt refuses. NEXT-RUNG TRIGGER:
// the shared execution lease the deploy-convergence design record specifies, where a release-consuming
// path holds the lease shared from its standing read through operation commitment while a converge
// holds it exclusively. That needs a shared/exclusive protocol, which an exclusive hold does not
// express, and it is a separate construction rather than a parameter of this one.

data deploy_transition_schema: String = "gunbc.deploy_transition/1"

// The durable records live beside the deployment, never inside the repository being transitioned.
// A file written under `repo_root` would be untracked residue in the tree the convergence reads
// back, so the convergence's own residue check would refuse on the artifact protecting it.
//
// ONE TRANSITION CONTEXT, BOTH RECORDS SEALED STORES IN IT. Every location a transition touches comes
// from one gunbc.deploy_transition_store TransitionContext, minted from the instance alone: the
// inhibition's store (declared once, transition_inhibition_slots) and the lease's store (declared once,
// gunbc.deploy_transition_lease_store transition_lease_slots, a hold on
// gunbc.durable_exclusive_hold_file_store). The guard is bound to its context and carries the hold it
// stands for, and no function below takes a root, a store beside a guard, or an instance; this module
// calls no filesystem operation itself.
//
// THE LEASE HAS NO NON-HOLDER RELEASE. ClearedByEvidence and ClearedByAbandonment below clear the
// inhibition and leave the lease held: the hold library's non-holder release is generic in the
// observation it trusts and its roster is frozen (stern-ram-763, 2026-10-25), so the lease does not
// consume it, and the residual is gunbc.recurring_failure_mode
// a_generic_release_assess_accepts_evidence_its_caller_authored. A TransitionResidue is read from the
// context's own inhibition store, but its observed head is the caller's string, so it is no release
// basis for the lease either. No production ingress acquires the lease today
// (gunbc.fleet.fleet_workflow_steps records the binding as a separate change), so a lease left held by
// a converge that died blocks only that future ingress. LINE-STOP: an ingress that binds
// acquire_transition_lease before the residual's trigger lands.

// The subject of one transition: which repository, moving which ref, to which candidate. WHERE its records
// live is not part of it: that is the TransitionContext the guard is bound to.
type TransitionSubject {
  repo_root: String
  base_ref: String
  candidate: String
}

// WHO holds it. `invocation` distinguishes two runs by the same actor -- without it, a second
// converge by the same principal could not tell its own stale record from a live one.
type TransitionActor {
  actor: String
  invocation: String
}

type TransitionRecord {
  actor: String
  invocation: String
  repo_root: String
  base_ref: String
  candidate: String
  phase: TransitionPhase
}

// THE PHASE IS A COPRODUCT, NOT A VALIDATED STRING. An earlier revision carried it as a `String` with
// two `data` rows for the admitted spellings and a check in the decoder, which is validation standing
// exactly where construction was available: it concedes that a record with a nonsense phase is
// WRITABLE, and every consumer then depends on the decoder having been the only way in. It is also a
// hand-rolled sum discriminator over a closed two-member vocabulary, which is what a coproduct is.
//
// The strings survive at the WIRE boundary and only there, because the record is serialized as JSON
// and JSON has no coproducts. `transition_phase_wire` and `transition_phase_of_wire` are that one
// boundary read in both directions, so a spelling no longer denotes a phase anywhere inside the
// model -- an invalid phase has no constructor rather than being refused by a check.
type TransitionPhase
  = TransitionInForce
  | TransitionPartialLeft

fn transition_phase_wire(phase: TransitionPhase) -> String {
  match phase {
    TransitionInForce => "in-force"
    TransitionPartialLeft => "partial-transition-left"
  }
}

fn transition_phase_of_wire(text: String) -> TransitionPhase? {
  if text == transition_phase_wire(phase: TransitionInForce) {
    Present { value: TransitionInForce }
  } else {
    if text == transition_phase_wire(phase: TransitionPartialLeft) {
      Present { value: TransitionPartialLeft }
    } else {
      none
    }
  }
}


// THE GUARD IS THE PROOF, AND IT HAS EXACTLY ONE MINT. `sole_constructor` means no other module can
// write this record, so an operation that takes a `TransitionGuard` parameter cannot be called
// without one having been acquired -- an unguarded transition has no constructor rather than being
// refused by a check somewhere downstream. That is the construction move: the caller sentence "this
// converge is serialized provided somebody acquired the lease first" is not enforceable, and this
// makes it unwritable instead.
//
// RUNG HONESTY. On the source-to-.dag acceptance path this is *structurally guaranteed*: a record
// literal for this type outside this module is refused. It is NOT structural on the emitted-Rust
// path, where DESIGN section 4b records that a sole_constructor type's emitted mirror is a public
// struct with public fields and is forgeable. Next-rung trigger: the emitted mirror carrying the
// construction confinement, which is that section's open item and not this module's to close.
//
// AND THE GUARD IS BOUND TO ITS CONTEXT. The lease it proves was acquired in `context.lease`, and every
// record the guard writes, marks, clears or releases is derived from that same context or from the hold
// below, never from a store handed in beside it: a guard acquired at instance A has no parameter through
// which to reach B.
//
// THE GUARD CARRIES THE HOLD IT STANDS FOR. `lease` is the lease store's own sealed proof, minted only from
// a committed acquire, so a guard exists only where a lease was actually taken; the holder's release
// presents it. It names its own store, which is `context.lease` by construction: the guard's one mint
// (lease_acquisition_of, reached only from acquire_transition_lease) acquires in that store. The hand
// lock's create outcome, whose `LeaseCreateSucceeded` minted a guard for any caller that wrote it with no
// lease anywhere, is deleted.
type TransitionGuard sole_constructor {
  context: TransitionContext
  subject: TransitionSubject
  holder: TransitionActor
  lease: TransitionLeaseHeld
}

fn transition_guard_record(guard: TransitionGuard, phase: TransitionPhase) -> TransitionRecord {
  TransitionRecord {
    actor: guard.holder.actor,
    invocation: guard.holder.invocation,
    repo_root: guard.subject.repo_root,
    base_ref: guard.subject.base_ref,
    candidate: guard.subject.candidate,
    phase: phase,
  }
}

fn transition_record_json(record: TransitionRecord) -> String {
  serialize_json(v: json_object(members: [
    json_kv(key: "schema", value: json_string(s: deploy_transition_schema)),
    json_kv(key: "actor", value: json_string(s: record.actor)),
    json_kv(key: "invocation", value: json_string(s: record.invocation)),
    json_kv(key: "repo_root", value: json_string(s: record.repo_root)),
    json_kv(key: "base_ref", value: json_string(s: record.base_ref)),
    json_kv(key: "candidate", value: json_string(s: record.candidate)),
    json_kv(key: "phase", value: json_string(s: transition_phase_wire(phase: record.phase))),
  ]))
}

// A STRICT FIELD READ, because the shared helper answers the empty string for a member that is
// present with the wrong shape. A record whose `candidate` is a number would then decode to "" and
// compare unequal to every head -- a refusal, but for a fabricated reason rather than the real one.
fn transition_record_field(document: JsonValue, key: String) -> String? {
  match json_object_unique_member(v: document, key: key) {
    JsonMemberFound { value: v } =>
      match v {
        JsonString { value: s } => Present { value: s }
        JsonNull => none
        JsonBool { value: _ } => none
        JsonNumber { lexeme: _ } => none
        JsonArray { elements: _ } => none
        JsonObject { members: _ } => none
      }
    JsonMemberAbsent => none
    JsonMemberDuplicated { count: _ } => none
    JsonMemberNotAnObject => none
  }
}

type TransitionRecordDecode
  = TransitionRecordDecoded { record: TransitionRecord }
  | TransitionRecordUndecodable { cause: String }

fn transition_record_missing_field_cause(key: String) -> String {
  join([
    "gunbc.deploy_transition: record field ",
    key,
    " is absent, duplicated, or not a string",
  ], "")
}

fn transition_record_from_document(document: JsonValue) -> TransitionRecordDecode {
  match transition_record_field(document: document, key: "schema") {
    Absent =>
      TransitionRecordUndecodable { cause: transition_record_missing_field_cause(key: "schema") }
    Present { value: schema } =>
      if schema != deploy_transition_schema {
        TransitionRecordUndecodable {
          cause: join([
            "gunbc.deploy_transition: record schema is ",
            schema,
            ", expected ",
            deploy_transition_schema,
          ], ""),
        }
      } else {
        match transition_record_field(document: document, key: "actor") {
          Absent =>
            TransitionRecordUndecodable {
              cause: transition_record_missing_field_cause(key: "actor"),
            }
          Present { value: actor } =>
            match transition_record_field(document: document, key: "invocation") {
              Absent =>
                TransitionRecordUndecodable {
                  cause: transition_record_missing_field_cause(key: "invocation"),
                }
              Present { value: invocation } =>
                match transition_record_field(document: document, key: "repo_root") {
                  Absent =>
                    TransitionRecordUndecodable {
                      cause: transition_record_missing_field_cause(key: "repo_root"),
                    }
                  Present { value: repo_root } =>
                    match transition_record_field(document: document, key: "base_ref") {
                      Absent =>
                        TransitionRecordUndecodable {
                          cause: transition_record_missing_field_cause(key: "base_ref"),
                        }
                      Present { value: base_ref } =>
                        match transition_record_field(document: document, key: "candidate") {
                          Absent =>
                            TransitionRecordUndecodable {
                              cause: transition_record_missing_field_cause(key: "candidate"),
                            }
                          Present { value: candidate } =>
                            match transition_record_field(document: document, key: "phase") {
                              Absent =>
                                TransitionRecordUndecodable {
                                  cause: transition_record_missing_field_cause(key: "phase"),
                                }
                              Present { value: phase } =>
                                match transition_phase_of_wire(text: phase) {
                                  Absent =>
                                    TransitionRecordUndecodable {
                                      cause: join([
                                        "gunbc.deploy_transition: record phase ",
                                        phase,
                                        " is outside the declared vocabulary",
                                      ], ""),
                                    }
                                  Present { value: decoded_phase } =>
                                    TransitionRecordDecoded {
                                      record: TransitionRecord {
                                        actor: actor,
                                        invocation: invocation,
                                        repo_root: repo_root,
                                        base_ref: base_ref,
                                        candidate: candidate,
                                        phase: decoded_phase,
                                      },
                                    }
                                }
                            }
                        }
                    }
                }
            }
        }
    }
  }
}

fn transition_record_decode(text: String) -> TransitionRecordDecode {
  match parse_json_document(s: text) {
    JsonDocumentUnreadable { gap: g } =>
      TransitionRecordUndecodable {
        cause: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: record is not a JSON document -- ",
          json_document_gap_text(gap: g),
        ], ""),
      }
    JsonDocumentParsed { value: v } => transition_record_from_document(document: v)
  }
}

// ABSENCE IS ESTABLISHED BY A SUCCESSFUL LISTING, NEVER BY A FAILED READ, and getting this wrong is
// how a marker-based guard fails open in its steady state. `Filesystem.Read` answers success=false
// both for a path that does not exist and for one that exists and cannot be read; those two worlds
// have opposite correct actions -- proceed, versus stop -- so a fold that maps a read refusal to
// "absent" hands the belt a permit exactly when the marker it could not read is the one saying stop.
//
// The first cut of this module did that. The scenario that surfaced it -- a marker written by one
// principal and read by another -- has since been ruled out by a separate decision about WHO
// acquires the lease (the convergence unit, as the service user, as its first act, so writer and
// reader are one principal). The defect is fixed anyway and that is the point: a fail-open that is
// harmless only while a deployment decision stays put is not a wall, it is a coincidence.
//
// WHAT CLOSES IT IS THIS FOLD AND NOT A FILE MODE. Making the marker world-readable was proposed and
// REFUSED, on grounds worth keeping: it removes one CAUSE of unreadability and leaves the AMBIGUITY,
// so the belt still could not separate "no transition in flight" from "I could not look", and the
// next configuration change reopens the same fail-open through a different cause. Presence is
// instead decided by a successful directory listing, so "absent" is a positive observation rather
// than the residue of a failure, and every way of NOT establishing it -- the listing refused, the
// entry listed but unreadable, the bytes unreadable as a record -- lands in one indeterminate arm
// that refuses at the admission seam; the admission has no create to fall back on, so the read is its
// whole decision.
//
// WHY AN ACQUISITION ARGUMENT NEVER EXCUSED THE READ ARM, recorded because it is what made the defect
// survive a careful reading. While the lease was a hand lock it shared this fold, and the argument for
// it was true -- a read refusal is not proof of absence, and acquisition was safe regardless because
// its exclusive create decided and the read only NAMED the holder afterwards. That argument was scoped
// to acquisition and was read as though it covered the admission read too. The lease now reads its own
// slot through the hold library and no longer passes through this fold.
// ABSENCE IS THE SUBSTRATE'S TO ESTABLISH, NOT THIS MODULE'S TO ASSERT. The absent arm carries
// `FilesystemEstablishedAbsence`, whose only mint lives in `extdeps.filesystem.filesystem_io` and
// requires a listing whose success channel was true -- so this module cannot write an absence at
// all, let alone infer one from a failed read. That is the same rule this file used to hold as
// prose and then broke; the carrier is why it cannot break again here.
//
// THE FOURTH ARM IS THE SUBSTRATE'S AND IT MATTERS MORE HERE THAN ANYWHERE. A listing that did not
// name the marker joined with a read of that marker that SUCCEEDED is two observations at two
// instants disagreeing, and for this lane the subject of the disagreement is the inhibition itself:
// the marker is being written or removed WHILE it is being read. Collapsing that into absence is
// precisely the fail-open this module exists to close -- it would report ActuationPermitted at the
// exact instant a transition is mutating the marker.
type TransitionFileObservation
  = TransitionFileAbsent(FilesystemEstablishedAbsence)
  | TransitionFilePresent { record: TransitionRecord }
  | TransitionFileIndeterminate { cause: String }
  | TransitionFileObservationsDisagree { cause: String }

// THE ONLY THING THIS FOLD DECIDES IS THE DECODE. Presence, absence, unreadability and the
// disagreement are all decided by `filesystem_file_observation`; what is genuinely this module's is
// that a file which read cleanly may still not be a transition record, which is a fourth way to be
// indeterminate and is not a filesystem fact.
// A REFUSED SUBJECT IS THIS FUNCTION'S CALLER'S DEFECT, NOT THE HOST'S. `FilesystemFileSubjectRefused`
// lands in the indeterminate arm here because a transition that cannot be observed must not proceed
// either way -- but the cause names the ARGUMENT rather than the host, so the two remedies stay
// separable to whoever reads the refusal.
fn transition_file_observation_of(observed: FilesystemFileObservation) -> TransitionFileObservation {
  match observed {
    FilesystemFileAbsent(absence) => TransitionFileAbsent(absence)
    FilesystemFileIndeterminate { cause: why } =>
      TransitionFileIndeterminate {
        cause: join(["gunbc.deploy_transition: ", why], ""),
      }
    FilesystemFileObservationsDisagree { path: _, cause: why } =>
      TransitionFileObservationsDisagree {
        cause: join(["gunbc.deploy_transition: ", why], ""),
      }
    FilesystemFileSubjectRefused { directory: d, name: n, cause: why } =>
      TransitionFileIndeterminate {
        cause: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: the marker ", n, " was requested under ", d,
          ", which is not a directory subject, so the supplied outcomes establish no observation of it -- ", why,
        ], ""),
      }
    FilesystemFileRead { path: p, content: c } =>
      match transition_record_decode(text: c) {
        TransitionRecordUndecodable { cause: why } =>
          TransitionFileIndeterminate {
            cause: join([why, " (at ", p, ")"], ""),
          }
        TransitionRecordDecoded { record: r } => TransitionFilePresent { record: r }
      }
  }
}

fn observe_transition_inhibition(inhibition: TransitionInhibitionStore) -> TransitionFileObservation {
  transition_file_observation_of(observed: transition_inhibition_observe(store: inhibition))
}

// Where an inhibition refusal points an operator. The store carries no path, so the instance names
// it; a host error from the store's own write already names the path.
fn inhibition_location(inhibition: TransitionInhibitionStore) -> String {
  join(["the transition inhibition of instance ", inhibition.instance as String], "")
}

type LeaseAcquisition
  = LeaseAcquired(TransitionGuard)
  | LeaseHeldBy { holder: String }
  | LeaseHeldUndecodable { cause: String }
  | LeaseAcquireLost { cause: String }
  | LeaseUnobtainable { cause: String }

// THE FIVE ARMS ARE FIVE DIFFERENT FACTS, decided by the lease store from the hold library's own
// typed outcome. An acquire that committed is the only admission. A lease held by another names the
// holder, and the caller retries later. A slot that does not decode refuses without naming a holder,
// because a corrupt lease is not a free lease. An acquire that lost a race after its own read is
// contention, and the caller re-observes. A store that could not be read or written is neither held
// nor free -- permission, a missing root, a full disk -- and gets its own arm rather than borrowing
// one of the others.
//
// NOTE WHAT IS DELIBERATELY MISSING: there is no timeout arm and no age anywhere in the hold. A lease
// that expires on a clock is a lease that a slow transition loses while it is still mutating, and the
// second acquirer would then be admitted into a live critical section by the very mechanism meant to
// serialize it. Staleness is resolved by EVIDENCE, never by duration.
//
// AND THERE IS NO PUBLIC GUARD MINT OVER AN OUTCOME. The guard's acquired arm used to live here as
// `lease_acquisition_of(context, subject, holder, outcome)`, taking the acquire outcome as an argument: any
// module could write `TransitionLeaseAcquired { held: <whatever held it could get its hands on> }` and be handed
// a TransitionGuard for whatever context, subject and holder it named beside it -- a second laundering route,
// one layer up from the store's. It is deleted. The only mint of a guard is inline in acquire_transition_lease
// below, over the outcome that call's own transition_lease_acquire returned, so a guard exists only where a
// committed acquire happened in the context's own lease store.

// THE FOUR REFUSED ARMS, which need no context: a refusal carries no held proof, so this fold cannot mint a
// guard and is decided by the store's refusal alone.
fn lease_acquisition_refused(refusal: TransitionLeaseRefusal) -> LeaseAcquisition {
  match refusal {
    TransitionLeaseOccupied { holder: who, generation: _ } => LeaseHeldBy { holder: who }
    TransitionLeaseUndecodable { detail: why } =>
      LeaseHeldUndecodable { cause: join(["gunbc.deploy_transition: ", why], "") }
    TransitionLeaseLost { detail: why } =>
      LeaseAcquireLost { cause: join(["gunbc.deploy_transition: ", why], "") }
    TransitionLeaseUnavailable { detail: why } =>
      LeaseUnobtainable { cause: join(["gunbc.deploy_transition: ", why], "") }
  }
}

// THE HOLDER'S IDENTITY IN THE HOLD, derived from the actor this module already names: the actor and
// the invocation as a JSON array, so the two are recovered unambiguously whatever either contains and
// two different actors never spell one owner. The hold library compares owners with its one equality.
fn transition_lease_owner(holder: TransitionActor) -> NonEmptyStr {
  serialize_json(v: JsonArray { elements: [json_string(s: holder.actor), json_string(s: holder.invocation)] }) as NonEmptyStr
}

// THE LEASE IS THE CONTEXT'S, AND THIS IS THE ONLY MINT OF A GUARD. Acquire takes the context and no store: the
// lease acquired is `context.lease`, so the guard names one instance in both its context and its hold.
//
// THE ADMITTING ARM IS INLINED HERE ON PURPOSE, beside the transition_lease_acquire call whose return value it
// reads. The guard is paired with this call's context, subject and holder only where the outcome is THIS call's
// own acquired arm; the refusal arms carry no proof and fold through the public refusal-only fold. No callable in
// this module or any other takes an acquire outcome and returns a guard, so an authored
// `TransitionLeaseAcquired { held: ... }` -- or a genuine one taken from some other acquire -- reaches nothing.
fn acquire_transition_lease(
  context: TransitionContext,
  subject: TransitionSubject,
  holder: TransitionActor,
) -> LeaseAcquisition {
  match transition_lease_acquire(store: context.lease, owner: transition_lease_owner(holder: holder)) {
    TransitionLeaseAcquired { held: h } =>
      LeaseAcquired(TransitionGuard { context: context, subject: subject, holder: holder, lease: h })
    TransitionLeaseRefused { refusal: r } => lease_acquisition_refused(refusal: r)
  }
}

type TransitionActuation
  = TransitionActuationCompleted { detail: String }
  | TransitionActuationRefused { cause: String }

// SETTING THE INHIBITION IS ITSELF AN EXCLUSIVE CREATE, for the same reason the lease is: a residue
// left by a converge that died is the only durable evidence that a partial transition happened, and
// an ordinary write would erase exactly that. O_EXCL makes the overwrite unrepresentable rather than
// checked -- a check-then-write would be safe here, since this function takes a `TransitionGuard`
// and the lease is already held, but safe-because-of-a-caller is the sentence construction exists to
// delete.
//
// MODE IS AN ACCESS QUESTION AND NOT A SAFETY MECHANISM (operator ruling, 2026-08-28). An earlier
// revision of this function wrote the marker world-readable so an actuator running as a different
// principal could read it. That was refused on the right grounds: it removes one CAUSE of
// unreadability and leaves the AMBIGUITY, so the belt still could not separate "no transition in
// flight" from "I could not look", and the next configuration change reopens the same fail-open
// through a different cause. The observation split above is the fix; the mode is not. The ruling
// that makes 0600 correct in fact is separate and is about WHO WRITES: the lease is acquired by the
// convergence unit as the service user, as its first act, so writer and reader are one principal.
//
// AND THE WRITE IS READ BACK. A successful write means the host accepted the bytes, never that the
// marker now reads as the record intended -- the same distinction the convergence this protects
// makes about its own steps.
fn set_transition_inhibition(guard: TransitionGuard) -> TransitionActuation {
  let inhibition = guard.context.inhibition
  let location = inhibition_location(inhibition: inhibition)
  match transition_inhibition_create(store: inhibition, content: transition_record_json(
    record: transition_guard_record(guard: guard, phase: TransitionInForce),
  )) {
    TransitionMarkerWriteRefused { detail: create_error } =>
      TransitionActuationRefused {
        cause: set_inhibition_refusal_cause(
          location: location,
          create_error: create_error,
          observed: observe_transition_inhibition(inhibition: inhibition),
        ),
      }
    TransitionMarkerWritten =>
      match observe_transition_inhibition(inhibition: inhibition) {
        TransitionFilePresent { record: back } =>
          if transition_record_same_subject(
            a: back,
            b: transition_guard_record(guard: guard, phase: TransitionInForce),
          ) {
            TransitionActuationCompleted { detail: location }
          } else {
            TransitionActuationRefused {
              cause: join([
                "gunbc.deploy_transition: ",
                location,
                " read back names ",
                back.actor,
                "/",
                back.invocation,
                " rather than this transition; something else is writing this marker",
              ], ""),
            }
          }
        TransitionFileAbsent(absence) =>
          TransitionActuationRefused {
            cause: join([
              "gunbc.deploy_transition: the inhibition write succeeded and the marker is not there on readback -- ",
              filesystem_established_absence_detail(absence: absence),
            ], ""),
          }
        TransitionFileObservationsDisagree { cause: why } =>
          TransitionActuationRefused {
            cause: join([
              "gunbc.deploy_transition: the inhibition write succeeded and the readback disagreed with the listing -- ",
              why,
            ], ""),
          }
        TransitionFileIndeterminate { cause: why } =>
          TransitionActuationRefused {
            cause: join([
              "gunbc.deploy_transition: the inhibition write succeeded and could not be read back -- ",
              why,
            ], ""),
          }
      }
  }
}

// A CREATE THAT REFUSED HAS THREE READINGS AND THE OBSERVATION SEPARATES THEM: a prior transition
// stands here and is named, something stands here that cannot be read, or nothing stands here and
// the directory itself refused the write. The third is a host problem and the first is a clearance
// problem, and an operator sent to the wrong one loses the time this whole lane is about.
fn set_inhibition_refusal_cause(
  location: String,
  create_error: String,
  observed: TransitionFileObservation,
) -> String {
  match observed {
    TransitionFilePresent { record: prior } =>
      join([
        "gunbc.deploy_transition: ",
        location,
        " already stands for ",
        prior.actor,
        "/",
        prior.invocation,
        " at candidate ",
        prior.candidate,
        " (",
        transition_phase_wire(phase: prior.phase),
        "); it must be resolved through the clearance path, never overwritten",
      ], "")
    TransitionFileIndeterminate { cause: why } =>
      join([
        "gunbc.deploy_transition: ",
        location,
        " could not be created and what stands there could not be established -- ",
        why,
      ], "")
    TransitionFileObservationsDisagree { cause: why } =>
      join([
        "gunbc.deploy_transition: ",
        location,
        " could not be created and what stands there is being written or removed while it is read -- ",
        why,
      ], "")
    TransitionFileAbsent(absence) =>
      join([
        "gunbc.deploy_transition: ",
        location,
        " could not be created and ",
        filesystem_established_absence_detail(absence: absence),
        ", so the directory refused the write -- ",
        create_error,
      ], "")
  }
}

// A PARTIAL TRANSITION REWRITES THE PHASE AND DOES NOT CLEAR ANYTHING. Delete-then-write would open
// a window in which the inhibition is absent while the tree is at its most dangerous, so the
// existing record is replaced in place by an ordinary write -- the one place this module writes
// without O_EXCL, and it is safe exactly because the guard proves this process still holds the
// lease. The lease is NOT released afterwards either: a partial transition is not a finished
// critical section, and handing the next converge a free lease over an advanced ref and an
// untransitioned tree is the interleaving this module exists to prevent.
fn mark_transition_partial(guard: TransitionGuard, detail: String) -> TransitionActuation {
  let inhibition = guard.context.inhibition
  match transition_inhibition_replace(store: inhibition, content: transition_record_json(
    record: transition_guard_record(guard: guard, phase: TransitionPartialLeft),
  )) {
    TransitionMarkerWritten => TransitionActuationCompleted { detail: detail }
    TransitionMarkerWriteRefused { detail: why } =>
      TransitionActuationRefused {
        cause: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: partial-transition record could not be written to ",
          inhibition_location(inhibition: inhibition),
          " at ",
          why,
        ], ""),
      }
  }
}

type ActuationStanding
  = ActuationPermitted
  | ActuationInhibited { record: TransitionRecord }
  | ActuationStandingUnreadable { cause: String }

// THE DISAGREEMENT ARM REFUSES, AND IT IS THE ARM THIS SEAM MOST NEEDED. A listing that did not
// name the marker joined with a read of it that SUCCEEDED means the marker is being written or
// removed at the instant the belt asked. Under the previous three-state fold that case rendered as
// absence, and absence renders as ActuationPermitted -- so the belt would have been admitted at
// precisely the moment a transition was mutating the inhibition. It is NOT folded into
// ActuationStandingUnreadable: "I could not look" is a host fault and "the subject moved while I
// looked" is contention with the transition this module serializes against, and an operator sent to
// the wrong one loses the time this lane exists to protect.
fn actuation_standing_of(observed: TransitionFileObservation) -> ActuationStanding {
  match observed {
    TransitionFileAbsent(_) => ActuationPermitted
    TransitionFilePresent { record: r } => ActuationInhibited { record: r }
    TransitionFileIndeterminate { cause: why } => ActuationStandingUnreadable { cause: why }
    TransitionFileObservationsDisagree { cause: why } =>
      ActuationStandingUnreadable { cause: why }
  }
}

// THE STANDING TAKES THE MINT'S READING, because every caller holds an instance and nothing else, and
// an unready store is a standing too: an instance root that does not list establishes no absence, so it
// is unreadable and refuses -- the answer the unsealed read gave for the same root.
fn transition_actuation_standing(inhibition: TransitionInhibitionStoreReading) -> ActuationStanding {
  match inhibition {
    TransitionInhibitionStoreUnready { detail: d } =>
      ActuationStandingUnreadable { cause: join(["gunbc.deploy_transition: ", d], "") }
    TransitionInhibitionStoreReady { store: s } =>
      actuation_standing_of(observed: observe_transition_inhibition(inhibition: s))
  }
}

type ActuationAdmission
  = ActuationAdmitted
  | ActuationRefused { reason: String }

// THE ONLY ADMITTING ARM IS `ActuationPermitted`. Unreadable refuses, and that direction is the
// whole point: a release-consuming actuator that proceeded because it could not tell would act on a
// tree that is mid-transition precisely when the evidence is damaged.
fn actuation_admission(standing: ActuationStanding) -> ActuationAdmission {
  match standing {
    ActuationPermitted => ActuationAdmitted
    ActuationInhibited { record: r } =>
      ActuationRefused {
        reason: join([
          "deployment transition ",
          transition_phase_wire(phase: r.phase),
          " on ",
          r.repo_root,
          " to candidate ",
          r.candidate,
          " held by ",
          r.actor,
          "/",
          r.invocation,
        ], ""),
      }
    ActuationStandingUnreadable { cause: why } =>
      ActuationRefused {
        reason: join([
          "deployment transition standing is unreadable and therefore refuses -- ",
          why,
        ], ""),
      }
  }
}

// WHO MAY CLEAR AN INHIBITION LEFT BY A CONVERGE THAT DIED, and on what evidence. This is the part
// that decides whether the mechanism is usable: an inhibition nobody can lawfully clear is an
// outage, and one anybody can clear is not an inhibition.
//
// The evidence is a REPOSITORY OBSERVATION, not an assertion and not a clock. A record naming
// candidate C is resolvable when the repository is observed AT C: whatever killed the process, the
// transition it was performing has in fact landed, so the state the inhibition was protecting
// against no longer exists. A record naming C over a repository at anything else describes a
// transition that did NOT land, and no amount of elapsed time changes that -- it refuses.
//
// A record whose own phase says `partial-transition-left` is never resolvable by observation alone
// even when the head matches, because the phase is a first-hand report from the process that was
// there: it reached the worktree transition and the worktree transition failed. A head that matches
// afterwards means something else moved it, which is more alarming than a mismatch, not less.
type ResidueEvidence {
  record: TransitionRecord
  head_observed: Bool
  observed_head: String
  observation_detail: String
}

// THE RESIDUE A CLEARANCE ACTS ON IS READ FROM ITS OWN CONTEXT. The record is never supplied: it is
// the inhibition this context's store holds, read here, so a residue for instance B cannot carry a
// record observed at instance A, and the clearance or abandonment it grounds acts on that one
// context. Sole constructor, non-generic, never a variant arm; `transition_residue` is its only mint.
// The head observation is still supplied by the caller (a typed repository observation is the hold
// library's follow-up, named in gunbc.durable_exclusive_hold_file_store).
type TransitionResidue sole_constructor {
  context: TransitionContext
  evidence: ResidueEvidence
}

type TransitionResidueReading
  = TransitionResidueObserved(TransitionResidue)
  | TransitionResidueUnobserved { cause: String }

fn transition_residue(
  context: TransitionContext,
  head_observed: Bool,
  observed_head: String,
  observation_detail: String,
) -> TransitionResidueReading {
  match observe_transition_inhibition(inhibition: context.inhibition) {
    TransitionFilePresent { record: r } =>
      TransitionResidueObserved(TransitionResidue {
        context: context,
        evidence: ResidueEvidence {
          record: r,
          head_observed: head_observed,
          observed_head: observed_head,
          observation_detail: observation_detail,
        },
      })
    TransitionFileAbsent(absence) =>
      TransitionResidueUnobserved {
        cause: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: no residue stands at ",
          inhibition_location(inhibition: context.inhibition),
          " -- ",
          filesystem_established_absence_detail(absence: absence),
        ], ""),
      }
    TransitionFileIndeterminate { cause: why } =>
      TransitionResidueUnobserved {
        cause: join(["gunbc.deploy_transition: the residue at ", inhibition_location(inhibition: context.inhibition), " could not be read -- ", why], ""),
      }
    TransitionFileObservationsDisagree { cause: why } =>
      TransitionResidueUnobserved {
        cause: join(["gunbc.deploy_transition: the residue at ", inhibition_location(inhibition: context.inhibition), " is moving while it is read -- ", why], ""),
      }
  }
}

type ResidueDisposition
  = ResidueResolvable { candidate: String }
  | ResidueTransitionIncomplete { candidate: String, observed_head: String }
  | ResiduePartialDeclared { candidate: String, observed_head: String }
  | ResidueStateUnobservable { cause: String }

fn residue_disposition(evidence: ResidueEvidence) -> ResidueDisposition {
  if !evidence.head_observed {
    ResidueStateUnobservable {
      cause: join([
        "gunbc.deploy_transition: repository head unobservable, so a transition residue cannot be resolved -- ",
        evidence.observation_detail,
      ], ""),
    }
  } else {
    match evidence.record.phase {
      TransitionPartialLeft =>
        ResiduePartialDeclared {
          candidate: evidence.record.candidate,
          observed_head: evidence.observed_head,
        }
      TransitionInForce =>
        if evidence.observed_head == evidence.record.candidate {
          ResidueResolvable { candidate: evidence.record.candidate }
        } else {
          ResidueTransitionIncomplete {
            candidate: evidence.record.candidate,
            observed_head: evidence.observed_head,
          }
        }
    }
  }
}

fn residue_disposition_detail(disposition: ResidueDisposition) -> String {
  match disposition {
    ResidueResolvable { candidate: c } =>
      join([
        "transition residue is resolvable: the repository is observed at the candidate ",
        c,
        " the record names, so the transition it describes landed",
      ], "")
    ResidueTransitionIncomplete { candidate: c, observed_head: h } =>
      join([
        "transition residue refuses: record names candidate ",
        c,
        " and the repository is observed at ",
        h,
        "; the transition did not land and clearing it would admit an actuator into a half-converged tree",
      ], "")
    ResiduePartialDeclared { candidate: c, observed_head: h } =>
      join([
        "transition residue refuses: the record declares a partial transition to ",
        c,
        " (worktree transition failed after the ref advanced) and the repository is observed at ",
        h,
        "; only an operator abandonment carrying that observation may retire it",
      ], "")
    ResidueStateUnobservable { cause: why } => why
  }
}

// THE OPERATOR PATH, AND WHY IT IS NOT AN ESCAPE HATCH. An abandonment does not permit actuation
// over a transition that is in force and it has no bypass semantics: it RETIRES a record, it is
// minted only with a fresh head observation in hand, and the record it writes names the operator
// and what they saw. Section 5 forbids a toggle whose only effect is to proceed as if the refusal
// had not fired -- this is the opposite shape, an actuation with its own evidence and its own
// receipt, and every other path still refuses while the record stands.
//
// RUNG HONESTY. This is *mitigatable*, not structural, and the reason is exact: nothing here
// observes whether the process named by the record is still alive, so the operator's assertion that
// it is gone is the load-bearing input and the model cannot check it. Next-rung trigger: a holder
// liveness observation on the deploy host -- a boot identity and process identity recorded in the
// record and re-observed at clearance -- at which point a live holder makes abandonment refuse by
// construction rather than by the operator being right.
type TransitionAbandonment sole_constructor {
  context: TransitionContext
  operator: String
  record: TransitionRecord
  observed_head: String
}

type AbandonmentMint
  = AbandonmentMinted(TransitionAbandonment)
  | AbandonmentRefusedUnobserved { cause: String }
  | AbandonmentRefusedNotOperator { actor: String }

fn mint_transition_abandonment(
  operator: String,
  operator_admitted: Bool,
  residue: TransitionResidue,
) -> AbandonmentMint {
  let evidence = residue.evidence
  if !operator_admitted {
    AbandonmentRefusedNotOperator { actor: operator }
  } else {
    if !evidence.head_observed {
      AbandonmentRefusedUnobserved {
        cause: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: abandonment refuses without a fresh head observation -- ",
          evidence.observation_detail,
        ], ""),
      }
    } else {
      AbandonmentMinted(TransitionAbandonment {
        context: residue.context,
        operator: operator,
        record: evidence.record,
        observed_head: evidence.observed_head,
      })
    }
  }
}

// The three lawful bases for removing the durable records, as one closed vocabulary. A caller that
// has none of these cannot express a clearance at all: `ClearedByHolder` needs the guard, which has
// one mint; `ClearedByEvidence` needs a residue, which is read from the context's own store;
// `ClearedByAbandonment` needs the abandonment, which has one mint of its own.
//
// EVERY BASIS CARRIES THE ONE CONTEXT ITS CLEARANCE ACTS ON, inside a sealed value minted from that
// context: the guard's, the residue's (whose record was read from that context's store), the
// abandonment's (minted from a residue). The clearance derives both locations from it and takes no
// location of its own, so a basis computed for instance A cannot remove instance B's inhibition and
// then A's lease.
type ClearanceBasis
  = ClearedByHolder(TransitionGuard)
  | ClearedByEvidence(TransitionResidue)
  | ClearedByAbandonment(TransitionAbandonment)

type ClearanceAdmission
  = ClearanceAdmitted { basis: String }
  | ClearanceRefused { reason: String }

// THE HOLDER'S OWN CLEARANCE IS CONDITIONAL ON A COMPLETE READBACK, supplied by the caller as the
// convergence verdict it already computed. A holder that clears on exit status alone would clear on
// "git accepted every command", which is not the same claim as "the repository now holds the
// intended state" -- and the tree this replaces was assembled entirely from commands that succeeded.
//
// AND CLEARING IS SUBJECT-MATCHED, NOT A BLIND DELETE. Every basis names the exact record it was
// computed against, and the record STILL ON DISK is re-read at clearance and compared to it. Without
// that comparison a late-finishing older deployment clears the marker a newer one has since set --
// the newer transition then runs with no inhibition at all, and the belt is admitted into the tree
// it is mutating. The mechanism is the same compare-and-swap discipline the base-ref update uses,
// applied to the durable marker: the expected prior value the actor observed is carried into the
// write, so a record that moved underneath refuses instead of being clobbered.
fn transition_record_same_subject(a: TransitionRecord, b: TransitionRecord) -> Bool {
  a.actor == b.actor
    && a.invocation == b.invocation
    && a.repo_root == b.repo_root
    && a.base_ref == b.base_ref
    && a.candidate == b.candidate
}

fn clearance_expected_record(basis: ClearanceBasis) -> TransitionRecord {
  match basis {
    ClearedByHolder(guard) => transition_guard_record(guard: guard, phase: TransitionInForce)
    ClearedByEvidence(residue) => residue.evidence.record
    ClearedByAbandonment(abandonment) => abandonment.record
  }
}

fn clearance_subject_mismatch(
  expected: TransitionRecord,
  observed: TransitionFileObservation,
) -> String? {
  match observed {
    TransitionFilePresent { record: r } =>
      if transition_record_same_subject(a: r, b: expected) {
        none
      } else {
        Present {
          value: join([
            "gunbc.deploy_transition: refusing to clear a marker that belongs to another transition -- the record on disk names ",
            r.actor,
            "/",
            r.invocation,
            " at candidate ",
            r.candidate,
            ", and this clearance was computed against ",
            expected.actor,
            "/",
            expected.invocation,
            " at candidate ",
            expected.candidate,
          ], ""),
        }
      }
    TransitionFileIndeterminate { cause: why } =>
      Present {
        value: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: refusing to clear an unreadable marker -- ",
          why,
        ], ""),
      }
    TransitionFileObservationsDisagree { cause: why } =>
      Present {
        value: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: refusing to clear a marker that is moving under the clearance -- ",
          why,
        ], ""),
      }
    TransitionFileAbsent(absence) =>
      Present {
        value: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: refusing to clear a marker that is no longer readable at clearance time; something else removed or replaced it -- ",
          filesystem_established_absence_detail(absence: absence),
        ], ""),
      }
  }
}

fn clearance_basis_admission(basis: ClearanceBasis, readback_converged: Bool) -> ClearanceAdmission {
  match basis {
    ClearedByHolder(guard) =>
      if readback_converged {
        ClearanceAdmitted {
          basis: join([
            "holder ",
            guard.holder.actor,
            "/",
            guard.holder.invocation,
            " cleared after a complete readback at ",
            guard.subject.candidate,
          ], ""),
        }
      } else {
        ClearanceRefused {
          reason: join([
            "gunbc.deploy_transition: holder ",
            guard.holder.actor,
            "/",
            guard.holder.invocation,
            " may not clear without a converged readback; the inhibition stays set",
          ], ""),
        }
      }
    ClearedByEvidence(residue) => {
      let d = residue_disposition(evidence: residue.evidence)
      match d {
        ResidueResolvable { candidate: c } =>
          ClearanceAdmitted {
            basis: join([
              "residue resolved by observation: repository stands at the recorded candidate ",
              c,
            ], ""),
          }
        ResidueTransitionIncomplete { candidate: _, observed_head: _ } =>
          ClearanceRefused { reason: residue_disposition_detail(disposition: d) }
        ResiduePartialDeclared { candidate: _, observed_head: _ } =>
          ClearanceRefused { reason: residue_disposition_detail(disposition: d) }
        ResidueStateUnobservable { cause: _ } =>
          ClearanceRefused { reason: residue_disposition_detail(disposition: d) }
      }
    }
    ClearedByAbandonment(abandonment) =>
      ClearanceAdmitted {
        basis: join([
          "operator ",
          abandonment.operator,
          " abandoned the transition to ",
          abandonment.record.candidate,
          " with the repository observed at ",
          abandonment.observed_head,
        ], ""),
      }
  }
}

// THE SUBJECT CHECK DOMINATES THE BASIS CHECK. A clearance whose basis is perfectly sound is still
// refused when the record it was computed against is not the record on disk, because in that case
// the sound basis describes a transition that is over and the marker describes one that is not.
fn clearance_admission(
  basis: ClearanceBasis,
  readback_converged: Bool,
  observed: TransitionFileObservation,
) -> ClearanceAdmission {
  match clearance_subject_mismatch(
    expected: clearance_expected_record(basis: basis),
    observed: observed,
  ) {
    Present { value: why } => ClearanceRefused { reason: why }
    Absent => clearance_basis_admission(basis: basis, readback_converged: readback_converged)
  }
}

// ORDER MATTERS ON THE WAY OUT AS WELL AS ON THE WAY IN. The inhibition is removed FIRST and the
// lease SECOND, so a process that dies between the two leaves a held lease over a converged tree with
// no inhibition -- the belt is admitted into a tree that is converged, and the next converge refuses
// on the lease. Releasing the lease first would leave the opposite: a second converge admitted into a
// repository the belt is still refusing, with nothing holding the section it is about to mutate.
//
// THE MARKER IS RE-READ HERE rather than at the caller, so the compare and the delete sit in one
// fold with nothing between them that a caller could reorder or skip.
//
// ONE CONTEXT, BOTH LOCATIONS: the inhibition and the lease are both derived from the basis's own
// context, so the two deletes act on one instance by construction.
fn clearance_context(basis: ClearanceBasis) -> TransitionContext {
  match basis {
    ClearedByHolder(guard) => guard.context
    ClearedByEvidence(residue) => residue.context
    ClearedByAbandonment(abandonment) => abandonment.context
  }
}

fn clear_transition_records(basis: ClearanceBasis, readback_converged: Bool) -> TransitionActuation {
  let context = clearance_context(basis: basis)
  let inhibition = context.inhibition
  match clearance_admission(
    basis: basis,
    readback_converged: readback_converged,
    observed: observe_transition_inhibition(inhibition: inhibition),
  ) {
    ClearanceRefused { reason: why } => TransitionActuationRefused { cause: why }
    ClearanceAdmitted { basis: b } =>
      match transition_inhibition_delete(store: inhibition) {
        TransitionMarkerWriteRefused { detail: why } =>
          TransitionActuationRefused {
            cause: join([
              "gunbc.deploy_transition: ",
              inhibition_location(inhibition: inhibition),
              " could not be removed at ",
              why,
              "; the lease is deliberately left held so nothing is admitted while the inhibition stands",
            ], ""),
          }
        TransitionMarkerWritten => release_transition_lease_after_clearance(basis: basis, cleared: b)
      }
  }
}

// ONLY THE HOLDER RELEASES THE LEASE. ClearedByHolder releases it through the guard's own proof of the hold.
// ClearedByEvidence and ClearedByAbandonment clear the inhibition on their basis and then REFUSE to release the
// lease, which stays held by the holder that is not the caller: a non-holder release is not modeled for this
// lease, a residue's observed head is the caller's string, and abandonment is not a lease-release basis until it
// binds a std.scoped_authorization AuthorizationDecision. The refusal says the inhibition was cleared, so the
// caller knows the one mutation that happened.
data transition_lease_nonholder_release_residual: String = "gunbc.recurring_failure_mode a_generic_release_assess_accepts_evidence_its_caller_authored"

fn release_transition_lease_after_clearance(basis: ClearanceBasis, cleared: String) -> TransitionActuation {
  match basis {
    ClearedByHolder(guard) =>
      match transition_lease_release(
        held: guard.lease,
        released_by: join(["holder-cleared ", guard.holder.actor, "/", guard.holder.invocation], "") as NonEmptyStr,
      ) {
        TransitionLeaseReleased => TransitionActuationCompleted { detail: cleared }
        TransitionLeaseReleaseRefused { detail: why } =>
          TransitionActuationRefused {
            cause: join(["gunbc.deploy_transition: inhibition cleared (", cleared, ") but the lease was not released -- ", why], ""),
          }
      }
    ClearedByEvidence(_) =>
      TransitionActuationRefused {
        cause: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: inhibition cleared (", cleared,
          ") and the transition lease is NOT released: evidence does not release a lease its caller does not hold; a non-holder release of this lease is not modeled (",
          transition_lease_nonholder_release_residual, ")",
        ], ""),
      }
    ClearedByAbandonment(_) =>
      TransitionActuationRefused {
        cause: join([
          "gunbc.deploy_transition: inhibition cleared (", cleared,
          ") and the transition lease is NOT released: an operator abandonment is not a lease-release basis until it binds a std.scoped_authorization AuthorizationDecision (",
          transition_lease_nonholder_release_residual, ")",
        ], ""),
      }
  }
}
